## Writing Your Own Derive

```xiom
// Define a custom interface
pub interface Validate {
  fn validate(self: &Self) -> Vec[Str];  // Returns validation errors
}

// Write a derive macro for it
#[derive_macro(Validate)]
pub fn derive_validate(input: TypeDefinition) -> Code {
  let fields = input.fields();
  var code = Code::new();

  code.add("impl Validate for ${input.name} {");
  code.add("  fn validate(self: &Self) -> Vec[Str] {");
  code.add("    var errors = Vec[Str]();");

  for field in fields {
    if field.has_attribute("validate") {
      let check = field.get_attribute("validate");
      code.add("    if !(${check}) { errors.push(\"field ${field.name} failed validation\"); }");
    }
  }

  code.add("    return errors;");
  code.add("  }");
  code.add("}");

  return code.build();
}
```

### Using the Custom Derive

```xiom
#[derive(Validate)]
pub type User = {
  #[validate(self.len() >= 3)]
  name: Str;

  #[validate(self > 0 && self < 150)]
  age: Int;

  email: Str;  // No validation
}
```

### When to Write Custom Derives

1. **Project-wide patterns**: serialize, deserialize, validate, log
2. **Domain interfaces**: every type needs EntityId, Timestamped, Auditable
3. **Code generation**: boilerplate that appears in many impl blocks
4. **Meta-programming**: type-level computations, compile-time reflection

### Derive vs Regular Macro

```xiom
// Derive macro: applied to type definition
derive!(MyType, MyInterface);  // Or #[derive(MyInterface)]

// Regular macro: applied to code
macro_rules! validate {
  ($struct:ident { $($field:ident: $type:ty),* }) => { /* ... */ };
}
```

Derive macros have access to the type's AST (field names, types, attributes). Regular macros have access to token streams only.

